## My answer to the prompt

Ubuntu alone doesn’t identify the GPIO hardware, so the pin numbers and GPIO API depend on the computer you’re using. The example below assumes a Raspberry Pi running Ubuntu, libgpiod v1.x, and GPIO offsets 17, 18, 22, and 23. It also assumes the L298N’s ENA and ENB jumpers are fitted, so both channels are enabled. **Do not connect motors to GPIO pins:** use a suitable motor supply for the L298N and connect the supply, driver, and computer grounds together. A 5 V motor may receive substantially less than 5 V through an L298N because the driver has a significant voltage drop.

```cpp
#include <gpiod.h>   // Provides the Linux GPIO character-device interface.
#include <iostream>  // Provides error-message output.
#include <unistd.h>  // Provides sleep().

int main() {
    // Open the GPIO chip device. The chip name and offsets depend on your board.
    gpiod_chip* chip = gpiod_chip_open("/dev/gpiochip0");
    if (chip == nullptr) {
        std::cerr << "Could not open /dev/gpiochip0\n";
        return 1;
    }

    // Get the four GPIO lines wired to the L298N input pins.
    gpiod_line* in1 = gpiod_chip_get_line(chip, 17); // Motor A input 1.
    gpiod_line* in2 = gpiod_chip_get_line(chip, 18); // Motor A input 2.
    gpiod_line* in3 = gpiod_chip_get_line(chip, 22); // Motor B input 1.
    gpiod_line* in4 = gpiod_chip_get_line(chip, 23); // Motor B input 2.
    if (!in1 || !in2 || !in3 || !in4) {
        std::cerr << "Could not get all GPIO lines\n";
        gpiod_chip_close(chip);
        return 1;
    }

    // Request the lines as outputs, initially LOW.
    if (gpiod_line_request_output(in1, "l298n", 0) < 0 ||
        gpiod_line_request_output(in2, "l298n", 0) < 0 ||
        gpiod_line_request_output(in3, "l298n", 0) < 0 ||
        gpiod_line_request_output(in4, "l298n", 0) < 0) {
        std::cerr << "Could not request all GPIO lines as outputs\n";
        gpiod_chip_close(chip);
        return 1;
    }

    // Set Motor A to one direction and Motor B to the opposite direction.
    gpiod_line_set_value(in1, 1); // Motor A: input 1 HIGH.
    gpiod_line_set_value(in2, 0); // Motor A: input 2 LOW.
    gpiod_line_set_value(in3, 0); // Motor B: input 1 LOW.
    gpiod_line_set_value(in4, 1); // Motor B: input 2 HIGH.

    sleep(2); // Keep that direction command for two seconds.

    // Set both inputs LOW on each channel to stop the motors.
    gpiod_line_set_value(in1, 0);
    gpiod_line_set_value(in2, 0);
    gpiod_line_set_value(in3, 0);
    gpiod_line_set_value(in4, 0);

    // Release the GPIO lines and close the GPIO chip.
    gpiod_line_release(in1);
    gpiod_line_release(in2);
    gpiod_line_release(in3);
    gpiod_line_release(in4);
    gpiod_chip_close(chip);
    return 0; // Report successful completion.
