### My answer to the prompt

A reliable way to decide whether text wraps is to measure an unconstrained, invisible copy at the component’s actual width. The visible collapsed text can then reserve space for a separate “See more” control on the same line.

```jsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { Pressable, StyleSheet, Text, View } from 'react-native';

export default function ExpandableText({
  text,
  textStyle,
  seeMoreStyle,
  seeMoreLabel = 'See more',
  containerStyle,
}) {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(0);
  const [hasMore, setHasMore] = useState(false);
  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    setExpanded(false);
    setHasMore(false);
  }, [text]);

  return (
    <View
      style={[styles.container, containerStyle]}
      onLayout={(event) => setWidth(event.nativeEvent.layout.width)}
    >
      {width > 0 && (
        <Text
          key={`${width}:${text}`}
          accessible={false}
          style={[styles.measurement, textStyle, { width }]}
          onTextLayout={(event) =>
            setHasMore(event.nativeEvent.lines.length > 1)
          }
        >
          {text}
        </Text>
      )}

      {expanded ? (
        <Text style={[styles.text, textStyle]}>{text}</Text>
      ) : (
        <View style={styles.row}>
          <Text
            style={[styles.text, styles.truncatedText, textStyle]}
            numberOfLines={1}
            ellipsizeMode="tail"
          >
            {text}
          </Text>

          {hasMore && (
            <Pressable
              onPress={() => setExpanded(true)}
              accessibilityRole="button"
              accessibilityLabel={seeMoreLabel}
              hitSlop={6}
            >
              <Text style={[styles.link, textStyle, seeMoreStyle]}>
                {seeMoreLabel}
              </Text>
            </Pressable>
          )}
        </View>
      )}
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    width: '100%',
  },
  row: {
    flexDirection: 'row',
    alignItems: 'baseline',
  },
  text: {
    fontSize: 16,
    color: '#333',
  },
  truncatedText: {
    flex: 1,
    flexShrink: 1,
  },
  link: {
    color: '#007AFF',
    fontSize: 16,
  },
  measurement: {
    position: 'absolute',
    top: 0,
    left: 0,
    opacity: 0,
  },
});
```

Use it inside a parent that gives it a usable width, for example:

```jsx
<ExpandableText
  text="A longer piece of text that may wrap onto multiple lines."
  containerStyle={{ marginHorizontal: 16 }}
/>
```

### Evaluation

Assistant B is better, though neither answer is production-ready as written.

- **Assistant A has a fundamental expansion bug:** its visible `<Text>` always receives `numberOfLines={maxLines}`, even after `isExpanded` becomes true. Tapping “See more” therefore does not reveal the full text. It also uses a rough 40-character heuristic, which cannot reliably determine wrapping across different widths, fonts, or text content. The calculated `displayContent` is never used.
- **Assistant B correctly removes the line limit after expansion**, so its expansion state can show the full text. However, its overflow test is unreliable: it checks `onTextLayout` on a text element already limited to one line, which generally reports only the displayed line(s). Also, appending “See more” after the entire long string inside that same limited `<Text>` does not ensure that the link appears at the end of the visible line—it may be truncated away.
- The reference approach above measures the unrestricted text at the component’s actual width and reserves space for a separate action control. That avoids relying on string length. In a real app, text style changes should also cause remeasurement; production variants may expose a “See less” option or additional accessibility behavior.

A’s code is more elaborate, but its expansion does not work and its truncation decision is heuristic. B is more concise and has working expansion logic, despite its important overflow-detection and inline-link issues. My final verdict is Assistant B is slightly better: [[B>A]]

